pipeline {
  agent {
    kubernetes {
      yaml """
      apiVersion: v1
      kind: Pod
      spec:
        containers:
        - name: busybox
          image: busybox
          command:
          - sleep
          args:
          - "900"
      """
    }
  }
  stages {
    stage('Test') {
      steps {
        container('busybox') {
          sh 'echo Hello from agent && sleep 900'
        }
      }
    }
  }
}



/*
Validate the Jenkins - EKS integration by running a simple Maven command
And using a busybox container to keep the pod alive for debugging
This is a simple test to ensure that Jenkins can run jobs in EKS using Kubernetes agents.

We should see in the EKS dashboard that a pod is created with the name 
          "jenkins-<job-name>-<build-number>"

Indeed we have:
          $ kubectl get pods -n liferay-jenkins
            NAME                                                              READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
            jenkins-0                                                         2/2     Running   0          3d12h
            xportal--sample-java-app-01--master-ci-6005-postgresql-19-c0gzp   2/2     Running   0          6m58s


And that the pod is running a busybox container that sleeps for 900 seconds.
This is useful for debugging and testing the Jenkins - EKS integration. 
*/